# Hajimi Audio Segmentation

When creating initial cuts for Hajimi shortcut/drop sounds, prioritize musical phrase boundaries over uniform duration.

- Do not cut all tracks to the same length. Each source track needs its own segment length based on its motif, hook, transition, or phrase ending.
- Prefer starts at natural phrase entries: intro downbeat, recognizable hook, vocal entry, percussion hit, or clear motif start.
- Prefer endings at phrase resolution: low-energy boundary, breath/space, cadence, reverb tail, or just before the next new phrase.
- Use silence/low-energy gaps as evidence, not as the only rule. Many Hajimi sources have no true silence; use relative energy dips plus musical context.
- Shortcut drops may be longer than typical UI sounds when the phrase needs it. A good range is roughly 3-13 seconds depending on the source.
- Fade-out is for smoothing a natural phrase ending, not for hiding a bad cut through the middle of a melody or vocal.
- Keep each shortcut’s sound identity stable: copy/cut/paste/save/find should map to distinct musical personalities, not random interchangeable snippets.
- Store decisions in `Samples/hajimi-local/segment-plan.json`; regenerate clips with `make recut-hajimi-one ID=<segment-id>` or `make recut-hajimi`.

Initial cut workflow:

1. Listen to the first 10-20 seconds of the source, or scan for a stronger hook if the intro is weak.
2. Pick a start at a clear musical entry.
3. Extend duration until the motif feels complete.
4. Use a short fade-in and a fade-out only at the phrase tail.
5. Validate by triggering the shortcut, not just by inspecting waveform duration.
